[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 395,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 228,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57912,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 5,
        "headingId": 228,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Angiolipoma is a subcutaneous tumour consisting of mature fat cells intermingled with small and thin-walled vessels, a number of which contain fibrin thrombi.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:05:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 396,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 229,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57913,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 5,
        "headingId": 229,
        "headingText": "<p>8861/0 Angiolipoma NOS</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-01-30T18:17:26"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 397,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 230,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57914,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 5,
        "headingId": 230,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2E80.0Y &amp; XH3C77 Other specified lipoma &amp; Angiolipoma NOS</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:05:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 398,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 231,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57915,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 5,
        "headingId": 231,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:05:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 399,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 232,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57916,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 5,
        "headingId": 232,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Cellular angiolipoma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:05:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 400,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 233,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57917,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 5,
        "headingId": 233,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The extremities (usually the forearm) are the most common site, followed by the trunk. Intramuscular haemangiomas and the so-called angiolipomas of parenchymal organs or of the CNS are different lesions, in that they contain larger vessels {29614355; 7356383}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:05:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 401,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 234,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57918,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 5,
        "headingId": 234,
        "headingText": "<p>Angiolipomas most frequently present as multiple subcutaneous small nodules, usually tender to painful. There is no correlation between the intensity/occurrence of pain and the degree of vascularity {7026412}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-01-14T14:36:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 402,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 235,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57919,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 5,
        "headingId": 235,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Angiolipomas are relatively common and usually appear in the late second decade or early third decade of life. There is a male predominance, and an increased familial incidence has been described (5% of all cases) {17511936; 7026412}. The mode of inheritance is autosomal dominant.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:05:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 403,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 236,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57920,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 5,
        "headingId": 236,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:05:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 404,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 237,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57921,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 5,
        "headingId": 237,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The vast majority of angiolipomas show a normal karyotype {9130991; 7515663}, but rare cases have been reported to show rearrangements of chromosome&nbsp;13 {29275363}. The majority (80%) have been reported to have low-frequency <em>PRKD2</em> mutations {28139834}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:05:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 411,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 244,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57922,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 5,
        "headingId": 244,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Angiolipomas appear as encapsulated yellowish to reddish nodules.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:05:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 405,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 238,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57923,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 5,
        "headingId": 238,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Angiolipomas typically consist of two elements: mature adipocytes and branching capillary-sized vessels, which often contain fibrin thrombi. The vascularity is more prominent in the periphery {7026412}. The relative proportion of adipocytes and vessels varies, and some lesions are almost completely composed of vascular channels. These cellular angiolipomas should be distinguished from angiosarcoma and Kaposi sarcoma {2294783}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:05:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 406,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 239,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57924,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 5,
        "headingId": 239,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:05:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 407,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 240,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57925,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 5,
        "headingId": 240,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:05:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 410,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 243,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57926,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 5,
        "headingId": 243,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> tender, often multiple subcutaneous nodules; mature fat with a variable amount of capillary vessels; fibrin microthrombi.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:05:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 408,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 241,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57927,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 5,
        "headingId": 241,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:05:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 409,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 242,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 57928,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 5,
        "headingId": 242,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Angiolipomas are always benign and show no tendency to recur.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:05:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]